WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.320
ED GLAESER: At their heart, cities are the absence of physical space between people.

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.180
Cities are density, proximity, closeness.

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:12.090
That proximity has plenty of upsides, like the ability to spread knowledge or

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:13.450
to facilitate trade.

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:17.670
But it also has downsides like too much crime and too much traffic congestion.

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:21.290
Perhaps the most important downside of urban density is the spread of disease.

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:24.230
And for thousands of years cities have been trying to limit

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:26.620
the awful costs of urban contagion.

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:29.880
If you live in a city in the west, you probably view contagious disease as

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:32.410
an annoyance, not a terrible life threatening scourge.

00:00:32.409 --> 00:00:35.820
We now have tools, antibiotics, vaccines, aqueducts that have so

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:38.340
far worked pretty well at preventing modern plagues.

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:42.370
The one big exception was AIDS, which caused enormous damage to urban America

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:47.090
during the 1980s and 1990s, and it still kills abundantly in Sub-Saharan Africa.

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:50.535
If you live in a wealthy country, you are now far more likely to die from cancer

00:00:50.535 --> 00:00:52.773
than cholera, but that wasn't true in the past.

00:00:52.773 --> 00:00:57.106
in 1900, a boy born in New York City was still expected to live six years less than

00:00:57.106 --> 00:00:59.140
a boy born in the countryside.

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:02.120
That was about the same life expectancy gap that existed

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:03.430
in the time of Shakespeare's London.

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:05.430
In this section,

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:08.750
we explore the contagious diseases that have often threatened cities.

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:12.930
Why should you care why every sip of urban water doesn't carry the flavor of death?

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:15.860
As AIDS reminds us, disease could strike again.

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:19.110
And we need to be ready to fight back with the knowledge of how we fought

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:20.080
disease in the past.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:23.340
You should also care about urban health because many of the developing world's

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:26.210
cities remain remarkably unhealthy.

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:29.990
And we're going to explore how to reduce illness in those places.

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:32.830
You should care about urban death in Africa primarily because they're

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:33.670
people, too.

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:38.210
But perhaps also because plagues that begin in Africa might not stay in Africa.

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:41.620
If we continue to treat waterborne illness with antibiotics

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:44.350
rather than with better water systems, we may end up

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:48.240
creating an antibiotic resistant superbug that could threaten all of mankind.

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:54.010
Finally, the story of urban health also matters because it helps us to understand

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:57.420
how cities can work together to solve their own problems.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:00.300
The story of the fight against cholera remains relevant for

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:04.790
thinking about the coalitions that could fight high housing costs today or

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:06.540
excessive transportation congestion.

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:09.590
Perhaps the most important advantage of urban proximity

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:13.080
is that it enables us to work together to make our world better.

